        // Steel per dollar of warship: hull structure and armour taken as 55 % of standard
        // displacement for an armoured cruiser and 45 % for an unarmoured destroyer. Treaty cruiser USS Pensacola (ordered 1924): cost
        // limit $11.1 M, 9,100 long tons standard; at 2011 prices (CPI-U, factor 12.85) about
        // 35 t of steel per $1 M, 360 t a level of $10.22 M. Fletcher-class destroyer (1942):
        // $6 M a ship, 2,050 tons standard; at 2011 prices (CPI-U 1942-2011, factor 13.8) about
        // 11 t per $1 M, 280 t a level of $25.55 M. Costs and displacements: the ships' public
        // records as summarized in Wikipedia (CC BY-SA). The 4,000 t a level used before were
        // 157 t per $1 M, a bulk carrier's ratio. A modern destroyer (Arleigh Burke IIA:
        // $1.84 bn at FY2011 prices, about 6,600 t light) is near 1.6 t per $1 M; the century
        // keeps the 1942 ratio, an upper bound after 1960 (docs/economy.pt-BR.md, "Aço").
